{
  "rules": [
    "Strong upward-trend underprediction: when the last 5–10 days show sustained gains, the model level is systematically too low by ~+2 to +10 €/t, with the bias growing with horizon (largest around days 10–25). Apply an additive uplift that increases with forecast step during strong rallies.",
    "Trend/acceleration lag: the model follows the direction but reacts too slowly to rapid repricing (it forecasts a flatter path while realized prices keep climbing). Increase slope/convexity when recent returns are positive and increasing (momentum/acceleration adjustment).",
    "Late-horizon reversal mis-timing: after a strong run-up, realized prices can roll over in the final week, but the model often keeps a smooth continuation or declines too early/too late. Add a regime switch: if prices are extended (e.g., far above 20–30d mean) then introduce a higher probability of a pullback specifically in days 20–30, not earlier.",
    "Volatility underestimation: realized paths show larger day-to-day swings than forecasts (model too smooth). Widen forecast distribution or add a volatility scaling factor, especially mid-horizon (days 7–20).",
    "Horizon-specific bias profile: near-term (days 1–5) errors are smaller; mid-horizon (days 6–20) shows persistent low bias in rallies; far-horizon (days 20–30) needs conditional mean-reversion only when the market is clearly overextended—otherwise keep elevated levels."
  ]
}
